.TH "UBERTOOTH\-FOLLOW" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-follow\fR \- active (BlueZ) CLK discovery and follow for a target address
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-follow [\-t BD_ADDR] [\-l LAP] [\-u UAP] [\-U <index|serial>]
[\-r file] [\-q file] [\-d file] [\-e errors] [\-a] [\-b hciN] [\-w delay]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
Follows a classic Bluetooth (BR/EDR) piconet for a given target address\. It uses a local BlueZ Bluetooth device to assist with clock discovery and an Ubertooth to follow the piconet's frequency hopping\.
.P
Name the target one of two ways:
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB\-t\fR is its whole address, \fBxx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx\fR, in either case of hex digit\. That is what the tool connects to, and it gives the LAP and UAP the board follows, so \fB\-t\fR needs neither \fB\-l\fR nor \fB\-u\fR\. Use \fB\-t\fR for a real target: it is the only form \fB\-a\fR works with, and the only one that connects to the target's address rather than a partly guessed one (see AFH below)\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR together are the lower and upper address parts, which is what \fBubertooth\-rx\fR reports for a piconet it has seen\. They are four of the six bytes of an address; the two missing ones are the non\-significant address part (NAP), which ordinary piconet traffic does not carry, and the tool fills them in as zero\. A page does not carry the paged device's NAP either, so that placeholder has nothing to do with whether the page this form makes to collect the clock offset succeeds, unless it happens to match the local Bluetooth device's own address, which a controller refuses to page (see AFH below)\.
.IP "" 0
.P
One of \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR without the other names no piconet, so it is refused and the missing half is named: \fBerror: \-l <LAP> requires \-u <UAP>\fR\. It used to be taken as no target at all, which read the address from the local Bluetooth device and followed that instead of what was asked for\.
.P
\fB\-t\fR with \fB\-l\fR or \fB\-u\fR is refused: the two are the same setting written two ways, and the tool does not reconcile them\. With no target at all, the address is read from the local Bluetooth device \(em note this may yield the wrong address in some cases, so naming the target explicitly is recommended\.
.P
The capture is gated by the board's squelch, which this tool does not set: it is the default \-120 dBm unless \fBubertooth\-util \-z\fR set another value while the board was idle, and the firmware resets it on every mode change, so it never carries over from an earlier capture\. See ubertooth\-util(1), SQUELCH\.
.P
Requires BlueZ (Linux only)\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-t <BD_ADDR>\fR
the target's whole address, \fBxx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx\fR, which gives the LAP and UAP to follow as well as the address to connect to\. Not with \fB\-l\fR or \fB\-u\fR, and given once\.
.TP
\fB\-l <LAP>\fR
target LAP (3 bytes / 6 hex digits), given once, and with \fB\-u\fR\. Not with \fB\-t\fR\.
.TP
\fB\-u <UAP>\fR
target UAP (1 byte / 2 hex digits), given once, and with \fB\-l\fR\. Not with \fB\-t\fR\.
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-V\fR
print the version: the host tools, then the firmware version and build of each attached board; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-r <filename>\fR
capture packets to a PCAPNG file\. A PcapNG writer seeks back over what it has written, which standard output cannot do, so \fB\-r \-\fR is refused: \fBerror: PCAPNG capture to stdout is not supported; use \-q \-\fR
.TP
\fB\-q <filename>\fR
capture packets to a PCAP file\. \fB\-q \-\fR writes the capture to standard output; see CAPTURING TO STANDARD OUTPUT
.TP
\fB\-d <filename>\fR
dump received symbols to the named file\. \fB\-d \-\fR writes them to standard output; see CAPTURING TO STANDARD OUTPUT
.TP
\fB\-e <0\-5>\fR
maximum access\-code errors to tolerate (default: 2)\.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR
enable AFH (adaptive frequency hopping); experimental, and needs \fB\-t\fR\. See AFH below\.
.TP
\fB\-b <hciN>\fR
local Bluetooth device to use (default: hci0)\.
.TP
\fB\-w <timeslots>\fR
USB delay in 625us timeslots (default: 5)\.
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
show help\.
.P
A record of a \fB\-q\fR or \fB\-r\fR capture carries the packet's own power and the board's estimate of the background on its channel, and the screen shows the two as \fBs=\fR and \fBn=\fR with \fBsnr=\fR the difference\. Where the board has admitted no quiet reading since it last started counting there is no estimate: the capture's noise\-power\-valid flag stays clear, Wireshark shows the field as \fBInvalid Noise Power\fR, and the screen prints \fBn=none snr=none\fR\. ubertooth\-rx(1) describes both figures and what the estimate rests on\.
.P
The board has one reading of the channel to work from, so both figures are of whatever is on it\. A transmitter louder than the packet, on the same channel at the same moment, is what the signal figure describes, and the packet is then part of the background\.
.SH "AFH"
\fB\-a\fR is experimental\. It makes the local BlueZ device connect to the target and asks the local controller for the AFH channel map of \fIthat\fR link, then follows the piconet keyed on the target's LAP/UAP\. The two are the same hop sequence only when the target is the Central of the piconet you want to follow, which \fBubertooth\-follow\fR cannot check\. Treat a capture made with \fB\-a\fR accordingly\.
.P
\fB\-a\fR reads the map over a connection to the target, and the tool asks for the target's own address before it will make one: \fB\-a\fR without \fB\-t\fR is refused before any file is created or device opened, with \fBerror: \-a reads the AFH map over a Bluetooth connection to the target, so it needs the whole target address: give \-t xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx\fR\. That is a requirement of the command line rather than of the air interface, as the next paragraph explains; whether it should stay is issue #316 \fIhttps://github\.com/SqueakyFloors/ubertooth/issues/316\fR\.
.P
A follow keyed on \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR instead connects to \fB00:00:<UAP>:<LAP>\fR, with the two most significant bytes zero, because ordinary piconet traffic does not carry them\. That guess is not what decides whether the page reaches the target: of the target's address, a page uses only the LAP and the UAP, as the access code, as the input that selects the page hopping sequence, and as the seed of the header error check and CRC of the FHS packet the pager sends (Bluetooth Core, Vol 2, Part B 1\.3, 6\.4\.6, 7\.1, and Table 2\.3), and the AFH map and clock offset reads that follow the page are keyed on the connection handle rather than on an address\. So a page that fails here has an ordinary cause: the target is out of range, it is not accepting connections, or the \fB\-u\fR given is wrong\. The one way the guess itself can stop the page is by matching the local Bluetooth device's own address, which a controller refuses to page\. What a real controller makes of the guessed NAP has not been measured on this project's bench (issue #699 \fIhttps://github\.com/SqueakyFloors/ubertooth/issues/699\fR)\. When the page does fail, the command stops, saying what to do about it:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: connecting to 00:00:33:44:55:66 failed: Host is down; \-l and \-u give four of the six bytes of the target address, so 00:00 is a guess: give the whole target address with \-t xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
The line offers \fB\-t\fR because it is the only way to take the guess out of the picture and is what \fB\-a\fR needs, not because the guess is why the page failed\. Give the target as \fB\-t\fR and the connection is made to the address as it is, so the line is then the reason alone, without the advice\. A page that fails without leaving a reason behind drops the \fB: <reason>\fR from whichever of the two lines it is\.
.P
The map that comes back is used only when it could be a hop sequence at all:
.IP "\(bu" 4
a failed read stops the command (\fBerror: AFH map read failed: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR), rather than following with whatever the map happened to contain\. \fB\-a\fR therefore does not work on a controller that does not implement HCI_Read_AFH_Channel_Map; run without \fB\-a\fR on one of those;
.IP "\(bu" 4
a reply of AFH off is followed without it: \fBthe remote device is not using AFH; following without it\fR\. Nothing is sent to the board in that case: starting the follow resets its hop mode and AFH map, so a map left by an earlier run cannot make it hop adaptively anyway\. An accepted map is applied once the follow has started, for the same reason (see ubertooth\-util(1), CARRY\-OVER BETWEEN RUNS);
.IP "\(bu" 4
a map with fewer than the 20 channels AFH requires, or none at all, is read again \(em three reads in total, 200ms apart \(em in case it has not settled\. If it still reports too few, the command stops and says so;
.IP "\(bu" 4
a mode byte that is neither off nor on is refused and printed\.
.IP "" 0
.P
Nothing is set on the Ubertooth until the map has been accepted, so a refused \fB\-a\fR leaves the board alone, and the connection the tool made to the target is dropped on the way out\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Follow a target whose address you know, writing a PCAP:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-follow \-t 11:22:33:44:55:66 \-q capture\.pcap
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Follow the same target with an AFH map (see AFH above):
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-follow \-t 11:22:33:44:55:66 \-a
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Follow a piconet by the LAP and UAP \fBubertooth\-rx\fR reported for it\. This connects to \fB00:00:33:44:55:66\fR, whose two most significant bytes are a guess\. If that page fails the command stops and asks for the whole address (see AFH above)\. Use \fB\-t\fR for a real target:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-follow \-l 445566 \-u 33 \-q capture\.pcap
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "A FOLLOW FROM START TO FINISH"
What a run looks like, from not knowing the target to a capture file\. It needs a Linux machine with a working local Bluetooth device, an Ubertooth carrying \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR, and a piconet that is carrying traffic throughout: a phone streaming audio to a headset will do\. A piconet that goes quiet stops giving the decoder anything to recover a clock from\.
.IP "1." 4
Check the local Bluetooth device is there and up\. With none, \fBubertooth\-follow\fR stops before it opens the board, naming the device it looked for\. \fBhciconfig hci0 up\fR brings one up\.
.IP "2." 4
Survey for the piconets around you, thirty seconds to start with and longer in a crowded room\. Each line is a LAP the board heard, with a UAP where it has worked one out\. That UAP is a guess from the packets the board decoded and a short survey can get it wrong, so give the survey time, or take the address from the device itself:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-rx \-z \-t 30
.fi
.IP "" 0

.IP "3." 4
Point the follow at the piconet the target leads: \fB\-t\fR with its whole address if you know it, or \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR with the four bytes the survey reported\. Either way the tool says what it is following before it opens the board, so a run against the wrong piconet shows in the first line:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-follow \-t 11:22:33:44:55:66 \-q capture\.pcap
ubertooth\-follow \-l 445566 \-u 33 \-q capture\.pcap
.fi
.IP "" 0

.IP "4." 4
Watch what the run prints\. The tool pages the target through the local Bluetooth device, reads the clock of the connection it gets, hands that to the board as the clock to hop on, and starts capturing\. Unlike ubertooth\-rx(1), it does not search the air for a clock: it is given one, so there is no acquisition to wait for and a follow that is working prints packets from the start\. A run that prints none has one of four causes: the piconet is not transmitting, the board cannot hear both devices, the \fB\-u\fR given is wrong, or the clock the board was given is off by enough to put it on the wrong channel, which is what \fB\-w\fR adjusts for\. A target that is not the Central of the piconet is a fifth with \fB\-a\fR (see AFH above)\.
.IP
The run ends when you interrupt it (Ctrl\-C), with exit status 0 and the capture written, so give it a length with \fBtimeout\fR when you script one\.
.IP "5." 4
Add \fB\-a\fR once the capture works without it, if the piconet uses adaptive frequency hopping and the follow is patchy (see AFH above)\. It needs \fB\-t\fR:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-follow \-t 11:22:33:44:55:66 \-a \-q capture\.pcap
.fi
.IP "" 0

.IP "6." 4
Open \fBcapture\.pcap\fR in Wireshark, which has dissected this format since 2\.2\. ubertooth\-rx(1) describes the per\-packet report the run printed, and the project documentation describes the capture format\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "WHAT A FOLLOW DOES NOT CAPTURE"
A capture holds the basic rate packets of one piconet, decoded as far as they can be without the link's keys\. Three things are not in it however well the follow goes:
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fBEnhanced data rate payloads\.\fR The board's radio demodulates Gaussian frequency\-shift keying at 1 Mbit/s, which is basic rate\. An enhanced data rate packet starts at basic rate, so its access code and header are decoded and the packet appears in the capture; its payload is sent in a phase\-shift keying the radio cannot demodulate, and that part is missing\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fBThe contents of an encrypted link\.\fR Encryption covers the payload, and the keys are agreed during pairing, which has usually happened long before a follow starts\. The capture holds the packets, their headers and their timing; what the devices said to each other stays unreadable\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fBAny piconet but the one named\.\fR A follow hops with one piconet's sequence, so traffic from everything else around it is off\-channel almost all of the time\. Capture a second piconet with a second board, not with a second filter\.
.IP "" 0
.P
Packets lost for other reasons are reported while the run goes; see GAPS IN A CAPTURE below\.
.SH "ARGUMENTS"
\fBubertooth\-follow\fR takes options only\. A word that is not an option, including a bare \fB\-\fR, is refused before any device is opened and named: \fBerror: unexpected argument '<word>'\fR, with exit status 1\.
.P
\fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR end the command only once the whole command line has parsed, so a stray word or a bad option value is reported even alongside them\. Both answer on standard output, so a command line with both prints both, the usage text last; the order they were written in makes no difference\.
.P
An option the tool does not know is refused the same way, as one line on standard error with no usage text after it: \fBerror: unrecognized option '\-Z'\fR\. An option whose value is missing reads \fBerror: option '\-l' requires a value\fR\.
.P
An option value must be a whole value in its range, or it is refused the same way instead of being read as 0, for example \fBerror: LAP must be 6 hex digits (got 4466)\fR and \fBerror: target address must be xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx (got 11:22:33:44:55)\fR\.
.P
Three more kinds of command line are refused before any device is opened, each with its one line:
.IP "\(bu" 4
any option given a value twice, \fBerror: option '\-t' given more than once\fR: the tool does not pick between two answers to the same question\. This is the rule for every tool; see ubertooth(7), OPTION VALUES;
.IP "\(bu" 4
\fB\-t\fR with \fB\-l\fR or \fB\-u\fR, \fBerror: \-t gives the whole target address, so it cannot be combined with \-l or \-u\fR;
.IP "\(bu" 4
one of \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR without the other, \fBerror: \-l <LAP> requires \-u <UAP>\fR or \fBerror: \-u <UAP> requires \-l <LAP>\fR: the two name a piconet only together\. \fB\-a\fR beside half a pair still reports \fB\-a\fR needing \fB\-t\fR, since completing the pair would not let \fB\-a\fR run\.
.IP "" 0
.P
\fB\-\fR means standard output only as the whole value of \fB\-q\fR or \fB\-d\fR (ubertooth(7), OPTION VALUES)\. Any other value is a file name, including one that starts with \fB\-\fR: \fB\-q \-x\fR writes the file \fB\-x\fR\. Standard output carries one stream, so \fB\-q \-\fR and \fB\-d \-\fR in one command are refused: \fBerror: \-q and \-d cannot both write to standard output\fR\.
.P
What the tool is following \(em the target address, or the LAP and UAP it read from the local device \(em is reported on standard error, with the rest of the tool's progress\. Standard output carries the decoded packets, and, with \fB\-a\fR, the accepted channel map \(em unless a capture has taken that stream, and then the map is on standard error with the progress; see CAPTURING TO STANDARD OUTPUT\.
.P
A refused command line creates and truncates nothing: \fB\-r\fR, \fB\-q\fR and \fB\-d\fR name their file while the command line is read, and it is opened only once every check has passed\. The board is opened and its firmware is checked before that, so a run refused for either reason leaves no empty file behind either\. A file that cannot be created is one \fBerror:\fR line naming it, for example \fBerror: /no/such/dir/dump\.bin: No such file or directory\fR, and exit 1\.
.SH "CAPTURING TO STANDARD OUTPUT"
\fB\-q \-\fR writes the PCAP capture to standard output and \fB\-d \-\fR the raw symbols, for a pipe into another program\. Either way the capture owns standard output for the whole run, so the per\-packet report is not printed; the packets themselves are in the capture\.
.P
What the tool is following, the clock the decoder recovers, the notes and the failures are on standard error and still arrive, and so is the \fBAFH map:\fR line of \fB\-a\fR, which is the answer to that option\.
.P
Redirect standard output to a new file or a pipe, not to one being appended to: on Linux the capture is opened again through \fB/proc/self/fd\fR, which truncates, so \fBubertooth\-follow \-q \- >> capture\.pcap\fR loses what the file held\.
.P
Binary on a terminal is a mistake rather than a stream anyone reads, so it is refused: \fBerror: stdout is a tty, refusing to write binary capture data to it\fR, exit 1\. Redirect or pipe standard output instead\.
.SH "GAPS IN A CAPTURE"
A capture that is losing packets says so\. The board holds captured packets in a queue until this tool reads them, and a queue with no room left loses the next packet\. The first time it happens the capture warns \fBwarning: the recording board's packet queue filled up and it dropped at least one packet; this capture has a gap\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many such gaps there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 gaps where the board's queue filled up and it dropped packets\fR\. The count is of gaps, not of lost packets: one full queue can swallow several packets, and the board sends no number\. Nothing says which packets are missing, so a capture with gaps is complete only between them\. The board reports a gap on the first packet that gets through after it, so a capture that ends in the moment after a gap does not report that last one\.
.P
A capture transfer error is reported the same way\. The chip's transfer controller can report an error on the channel a capture receives into: what the board was receiving at the time had arrived, so it is kept and marked, while some of its bytes may be wrong and whatever the fault cut short before it is lost\. The first mark warns \fBwarning: the recording board had a transfer error while capturing; what it was receiving was kept, but some of those bytes may be wrong and the capture may be missing packets around it\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 transfer errors where a packet may be wrong or missing\fR\. ubertooth\-util(1) \fB\-w\fR reports the board's own count of them since power\-on, over every capture the board has run\.
.P
Symbols the board wrote over are reported the same way\. The board receives into one block of symbols while its capture loop reads the one before it, and when a block finishes arriving before the loop has read that one, the new symbols go over the old ones\. The block is gone before anything looked in it for a packet, so this loss has no packet to point at and nothing in the output marks where it happened\. The first one warns \fBwarning: the recording board fell behind while capturing and wrote new symbols over a block it had not read yet; whatever was in the air then is missing from this capture\fR, and when the capture ends it says how many such places there were: \fBwarning: the capture has 3 places where the board wrote new symbols over a block it had not read\fR\. The count is of those places, not of the blocks lost at them: the board has one flag for this and no field to send a number in, so one place can stand for several blocks\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
A follow that was interrupted (Ctrl\-C) exits 0 with whatever it captured, and so does \fB\-h\fR\. A refused command line exits 1 (see ARGUMENTS), as does a file that cannot be opened and a target the tool could not set up a follow on\.
.P
A run stopped by Ctrl\-C before it had opened a board exits 0 as well: see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE\. That covers everything from the board being found onwards\. The adapter setup before it, and the page in particular, is the part of a follow that a signal ends the way it would end any other program: the page waits up to 25 seconds, BlueZ takes its own calls again when a signal cuts them short, and no handler of this tool's is installed yet, so Ctrl\-C there ends the tool at once rather than cleanly\.
.P
A page to the target that fails exits 1 as well, before any board is opened: the page is what yields the target's clock offset, and a follow of a named target without it would hop on the local device's clock instead\. A follow keyed on \fB\-l\fR and \fB\-u\fR pages a guessed \fB00:00:<UAP>:<LAP>\fR, so that line also asks for the whole address with \fB\-t\fR (see AFH above)\.
.P
A board that refuses the target's address or the clock reset exits 1 before the capture starts, with the request named on standard error\. Following anyway would mean a board that is not set up for the piconet: without the target's address it matches no packet, and with its clock never zeroed it hops on a clock the offset was not measured from\.
.P
Standard output stays empty in that case, and standard error names the request, why the board refused it and the libusb code, adding a note where there is something to try:
.IP "" 4
.nf
error: could not zero the board's clock: the board did not answer in time (libusb \-7)
note: if a mode is running, stop it with ubertooth\-util \-S; a board that does not answer at all usually resets itself within a few seconds, so run the command again, and unplug it or power\-cycle its hub port if it stays silent
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
That pair is the report, the same one ubertooth\-rx(1) and ubertooth\-scan(1) give: the request named as \fBset the Bluetooth address\fR for the address, and as \fBzero the board's clock\fR for the clock reset\. A request the board's firmware has not got is stalled, which reads \fBerror: could not zero the board's clock: the board's firmware does not support it (stalled, libusb \-9)\fR, with a note naming the board's firmware API version and the one these tools need (see ubertooth(7), FIRMWARE OF ANOTHER API VERSION)\. A board that took only part of either write gives the counts instead, as \fBerror: Bluetooth address write failed: board took 8 of 16 bytes\fR or \fBerror: clock write failed: board took 2 of 4 bytes\fR, and the follow stops on that one line\.
.P
A follow whose USB stream fails part\-way also exits 1: the board stopped answering, or dropped off the bus, so the capture stops there and holds only what arrived before it\. The failure is reported on standard error as it happens, as \fBerror: receive transfer failed: <reason>\fR\. A board that never hands the transfer back at all exits 1 too, reported by the \fBwarning:\fR line ubertooth(7) describes rather than that error\.
.P
A follow the board would not stop exits 1 as well: the request that takes the board out of its capture mode was stalled, so the board is left in that mode and no other tool can open it until it is reset or re\-plugged\. What was captured stays written out, and libubertooth names the refused request on standard error\.
.P
The \fB\-q\fR, \fB\-r\fR, and \fB\-d\fR files are created once the board has been opened and its firmware accepted, not before, and a run that is refused after that removes them again, so a refused follow leaves no empty capture behind whatever refused it\. For this tool the files are created later than it sounds: the adapter, the connection to the target, and the AFH read all come first, so a path that cannot be opened is reported after them\. A terminal on standard output is still refused while the command line is read, and so is every other fault in the command line\. A capture on standard output (\fB\-q \-\fR) is not a file this tool created, so there is nothing of it to remove: whatever standard output was redirected to is left as it is\.
.P
A path given to \fB\-q\fR or \fB\-d\fR that already holds something is emptied when the file is opened, and removing it on a refusal does not bring the old contents back; \fB\-r\fR refuses a path that exists rather than writing over it\.
.P
Only a regular file is removed\. A device, a pipe, or a symbolic link named as the destination is left where it is, along with whatever the run wrote through it, because the run did not create it\. A removal that will not go is reported as \fBwarning: cannot remove <path>: <reason>\fR and does not change the exit status\.
.P
A run that cannot allocate the piconet it follows exits 1 with \fBerror: out of memory\fR, before the adapter or a board is opened; \fB\-h\fR and \fB\-V\fR are answered first and so still work on a machine with no memory for one\.
.P
A capture that could not be started at all exits 1 as well, whichever step failed: the receive transfer libusb would not take, the host thread that collects the capture, or the request that starts hopping\. Standard error carries the one \fBerror:\fR line for that step, for example \fBerror: could not start the thread that collects the capture: Resource temporarily unavailable\fR\. The status is 1 in every case, never the failing call's own return value: a negative libusb code handed to the exit status is wrapped into a byte, so \fBlibusb \-9\fR reached a script as 247\.
.P
An adapter \fB\-b\fR names that does not exist, or that cannot be opened, exits 1 with one \fBerror:\fR line naming it, the same wording ubertooth\-scan(1) uses: \fBerror: Bluetooth device hci7 not found: No such device\fR\.
.P
\fB\-V\fR reads the attached board, so it exits 1 when a board is attached and cannot be read, or when the USB bus cannot be listed at all; see ubertooth(7), COMMON OPTIONS\.
